course: religion-ethics
lesson: 98
title: Honest with yourself: excuses, pride and the truth inside
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Excuses protect how we look but stop us fixing the real problem; "My dog ate my homework" sounds easier than "I forgot", but only the truth gets the homework done.
explain: Being honest inside means checking your own story: Rachel wrote that a fight was all her friend's fault, and reading her diary a week later she's not so sure.
explain: Christians practise looking back over the day: give thanks and notice where you went wrong; Ignatius of Loyola taught this prayer, the Examen, in the 1500s.
explain: Honesty inside also means facing your real reasons, your real feelings and the real numbers, like checking your actual screen time instead of guessing "an hour, max".
explain: The bravest honesty is saying "That's a good point" when the other side is right, or "I'm not sure" when you aren't.
fact: In Jesus's parable of the great banquet, the guests "all alike began to make excuses" (Luke 14:18); excuses stop us fixing the real problem.
fact: "Search me, God, and know my heart; test me and know my anxious thoughts" (Psalm 139:23).
fact: The Roman emperor Marcus Aurelius kept a private notebook, the Meditations, to be honest with himself; an honest diary helps you see both sides.
fact: "Let us examine our ways and test them, and let us return to the Lord" (Lamentations 3:40); Ignatius of Loyola, in the 1500s, taught the daily prayer called the Examen.
fact: Benjamin Franklin kept a chart of thirteen virtues and marked his faults every day.
fact: "As iron sharpens iron, so one person sharpens another" (Proverbs 27:17): ask people who care about you, and really listen.
fact: People who are new at something often think they are better at it than they are.
fact: "Do everything without grumbling or arguing" (Philippians 2:14): call a loss fair when it was fair.
fact: "Be careful not to practise your righteousness in front of others to be seen by them" (Matthew 6:1-4); being honest about your reasons helps you do good for good reasons.
fact: "Jesus wept" (John 11:35); feelings are nothing to be ashamed of, and sadness that's shared often eases sooner than sadness that's hidden.
fact: "Be very careful, then, how you live... making the most of every opportunity" (Ephesians 5:15-16); people often misjudge their own screen time, so check the real number.
fact: "Everyone should be quick to listen, slow to speak and slow to become angry" (James 1:19); olympiad judges give points for engaging fairly with the other side.
fact: "Do not spread false reports" (Exodus 23:1); honesty includes not claiming more certainty than you have.
fact: Slang check: "proper job" (Cornwall) means fine, all good; "yinz" (Pittsburgh) means you all; "well over it" means completely over it.
exercise: Arlo says, "My dog ate my homework," but he forgot. What is the honest version? | I forgot, sorry. Can I bring it tomorrow? | My little brother tore it up when I wasn't looking | The printer at home ran out of ink last night | I did it, but the wind blew it away on the way
exercise: Why do excuses feel easier than the truth? | They protect how we look, but stop us fixing the problem | Because teachers prefer excuses to the truth | Because excuses are always partly true | Because the truth is always harder to remember
exercise: In Jesus's parable of the great banquet, what did the guests do? | They all began to make excuses | They all came early and ate everything | They brought presents for the host | They argued about where to sit
exercise: Rachel reads her diary a week later and isn't sure the fight was all her friend's fault. What helps? | An honest diary that looks at both sides | Tearing out the page and forgetting it ever happened | Writing it again so it sounds better | Showing the diary to the whole class
exercise: Who kept a private notebook called the Meditations to be honest with himself? | Marcus Aurelius | Julius Caesar | King David | Benjamin Franklin
exercise: What is the Examen? | A daily prayer: look back over the day, give thanks, notice where you went wrong | A big exam at the end of the school year | A list of rules for church services | A prayer said only at Christmas
exercise: Who taught the Examen in the 1500s? | Ignatius of Loyola | Martin Luther | Saint Patrick | Saint Francis of Assisi
exercise: Ben thinks he's an amazing singer; everyone winces at karaoke. How can he find the truth? | Ask people who care about him, and really listen | Sing louder until people agree | Only ask people who always say yes | Never ask anyone, and just trust his own ears instead
exercise: What do people who are new at something often do? | Think they're better at it than they are | Know exactly how good they are from the start | Think they are much worse at it than they really are | Give up after the very first try, every time
exercise: Sasha shouts "That's not fair!" every time she loses, even when it was fair. What is honest? | Calling it fair when it was fair | Shouting louder until she wins | Saying it's unfair, just to be on the safe side | Never playing any games again
exercise: Josh helped the new kid partly so the teacher would notice. What does Matthew 6:1-4 say? | Don't do good things just to be seen by others | Always make sure the teacher sees your good deeds | Helping new kids is never really kind | Only help people when no one is around
exercise: Jago says "Proper job, I'm well over it" about his friend moving, but cries at night. What does "proper job" mean? | Fine, all good | A good job at work | Very sad | Not over it
exercise: What is honest with yourself for Jago? | Admit it's sad, to himself and someone he trusts | Keep saying he's fine until one day it comes true | Stop thinking about his friend for ever | Find a new best friend tomorrow
exercise: Mo spends about five hours on his phone but says "an hour, max". What should he do? | Check the screen-time number | Guess again, but a bit higher | Turn off the screen-time counter | Ask a friend to guess for him
exercise: In a practice debate Ava realises the other team's argument is better. What is honest and earns respect? | That's a good point | That's wrong and you know it | I'm not listening to you | Let's change the subject
exercise: Danny says, "Yinz all saw it, I never touched the ball!" but isn't sure. What is the honest version? | I don't think I touched it, but I'm not sure | I definitely didn't touch it, I swear on my life | You all touched it, not me | Whoever says I touched it is lying
test: Your mate says, "Excuses aren't lies, they're just reasons." What is wrong when the excuse is made up? | A made-up excuse is a lie, and it stops you fixing the problem | Nothing; all excuses are honest reasons | Excuses are only lies if the teacher checks | Excuses are fine if they're funny
test: "The dog ate it, swear down, Miss." What would happen if Arlo told the truth instead? | He'd likely get a chance to bring it tomorrow and be trusted more | He'd be sent home from school for ever | Miss would give him extra homework every day | Nothing; teachers don't care about the truth
test: A cartoon shows a girl writing in her diary, "Everything is always everyone else's fault." What is the honest diary habit? | Looking at both sides, including her own part | Writing only nice things about herself, so it's happy | Never writing about fights at all | Letting her friends write in it too
test: Ben shouts, "I'm the best singer in the world, the haters are just jealous!" What does Proverbs 27:17 suggest? | Listen to people who care about you; they help you grow | Never listen to anyone but yourself | Everyone who disagrees with him is just jealous of his voice | Sing louder so they can hear better
test: "That's not fair, the dice hate me!" shouts Sasha for the fifth time. What is the most honest reply to herself? | It was a fair game and I lost; next time | The dice really do hate her | Everyone else must have cheated when she wasn't looking | She should never play dice games again
test: Josh's friend says, "You only helped him to look good, so it didn't count." What is fair? | Helping was still good; being honest about reasons helps him do good for good reasons | He's right; the help counts for nothing | Josh should stop helping anyone at all | Josh should only help when nobody is watching
test: "Proper job, I'm well over it," says Jago, then cries at night. What would help more than pretending? | Telling someone he trusts that he's sad | Telling everyone he's never been happier | Never mentioning his friend again | Pretending his friend never existed
test: Mo says, "Screen time apps lie, it's like an hour, max." What would be honest with himself? | Check the real number and face it | Delete the app so he doesn't know | Ask his mum to guess for him | Say "an hour" until it becomes true
test: The judge asks, "Why did you say the other team had a good point?" Which is the best answer? | Admitting a good point is honest, and judges give points for it | I was trying to make them lose | I forgot what my point was | I wanted to finish early
test: Danny's mate says, "Just say you defo didn't touch it, they can't prove it." What is wrong? | Claiming certainty you don't have is not honest | Nothing; you should always say "defo" | He should say he touched it on purpose, to be extra honest | It only matters in professional games
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the principal disadvantage of an excuse? | It preserves the appearance of the speaker whilst preventing the remedy of the actual problem | It invariably results in a more severe penalty than the truth | It is always more difficult to formulate than the truth | It possesses no disadvantage whatsoever
exam: Identify the practice of prayer taught by Ignatius of Loyola, whereby the day is reviewed with thanksgiving and the noting of faults. | The Examen | The Rosary | The Angelus | The Litany
exam: Select the response most consistent with honesty where a speaker is uncertain whether he committed a foul. | The candid admission of his uncertainty | The confident denial of having committed the foul | The attribution of the foul to an opponent | The refusal to answer any question concerning the incident
exam: Ascertain which statement concerning the self-assessment of persons new to an activity is consistent with this lesson. | Such persons frequently overestimate their own ability | Such persons invariably assess their ability with perfect accuracy | Such persons invariably underestimate their ability by a great margin | Such persons are incapable of any form of self-assessment
